# PART 20: ONLINE AND OFFLINE FUNCTIONALITY

This is one of the most important requirements.

Build the application using an offline-first architecture.

### Online mode

When connected to the internet:
- Connect to the central backend.
- Read and write central database records.
- Synchronize employee records.
- Synchronize stock.
- Synchronize payroll.
- Synchronize finance.
- Synchronize documents.
- Synchronize notifications.
- Synchronize messages.

### Offline mode

When the internet is unavailable:
- Open the application.
- Log in using securely provisioned offline credentials.
- Access permitted locally cached information.
- Register attendance where authorized.
- Record goods received.
- Record goods issued.
- Register inventory changes.
- Create permitted documents.
- Save offline transactions.
- View locally available reports.
- Queue changes for synchronization.
- used all funcation of the application

### Synchronization engine

Implement:
- IndexedDB local database.
- Background synchronization.
- Pending transaction queue.
- Unique transaction IDs.
- Conflict detection.
- Duplicate prevention.
- Retry mechanism.
- Synchronization status.
- Failed synchronization reports.
- Manual synchronization button.
- Last synchronization timestamp.

Never silently overwrite conflicting records.

For financial, payroll and inventory records, use explicit conflict resolution and authorized approval.

Important: Offline changes on different devices cannot automatically synchronize with each other while disconnected. 

Sensitive information, especially payroll and banking records, must not be available offline unless specifically authorized and securely protected.

